#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ot-run: run a heavy command on the least CPU-loaded fleet host that can admit it (admission is by memory only).

  ot-run --mem GB [--threads N] [--dir REMOTE_DIR] [--disk GB] [--hosts a,b] [--pick] [--stage PATH ...] -- <command...>
  ot-run --mem GB --job SPEC.json [--hosts a,b] ... -- <command...>
                           SEALED JOB (ISOLATE-1, OWNER 2026-11-09 "yes, isolate the jobs. simply delete the finished job
                           folder"): the command runs through ot-job on the chosen host, in its own job dir with only the
                           declared inputs of the spec (read-only, from the host store) and its work dir; any other host
                           path fails. At exit the outputs the spec names are published to the store and the job dir is
                           deleted. The spec travels inline (base64); no --dir is needed, and SPILL is off.
  ot-run --selftest [HOST]   proves on HOST (default: every host) that a command runs and returns ITS exit status, and
                             that a request that cannot be admitted fails non-zero with NOT RUN (never a silent 0)
  ot-run --status          one line: per host, the largest request it admits now, MemAvailable, load1/cores, waiters;
                           and the host the next 16-thread job would get

Asks every host for `ot-heavy-reconcile --free` (free_gb = the largest request `ot-heavy` admits there now, by the same
arithmetic; queued waiters are reported separately), drops hosts with less than --disk GB (default 20) free under --dir (or
where --dir does not exist), and among the hosts that can admit --mem takes the one with the lowest
(load1 + threads of the units started in the last 90 s + this job's threads) / cores  (v4, owner 2026-10-13 17:55: the
128-core hosts fill evenly before either passes 1.0 x cores).  A host with fewer than 48 cores never gets a job of more
than 8 threads (--threads, default 16) while a larger host that can admit it still has cores.  Then it runs
ssh <host> "cd DIR && ot-heavy --mem GB -- <command>".
It syncs nothing: the inputs must already be on the host (name them with --dir so only hosts that have them qualify).
--pick prints an eligible host and the table and runs nothing.  stdin/stdout/exit status are the remote command's.
NO LOAD HOLD (v6, owner 2026-10-18 ~01:05 "NEVER hold jobs on CPU load; keep the fleet busy"; memory is the only
admission limit): CPU load only ORDERS the hosts that admit a request now (least loaded first); it never holds, delays or
excludes a job.  The v5 client-side hold (load1 > 1.5 x cores) is gone; OT_RUN_NO_LOAD_HOLD is accepted and ignored.
Probes report load as cpu_load1 (load1 from older probes is read too).
REBALANCE (v6): when no host admits the request now and more than one host qualifies (by --dir / --disk / --hosts),
the job waits on the host with the most planned free memory (or on the chosen host, when it lost an admission race)
for at most REQUEUE_S (300 s), then is cancelled there
(never started) and placed again on whichever qualifying host admits it, so a queue on one host drains into memory
freed on another.  Data locality is kept: only hosts that have --dir qualify; --hosts pins.  --max-wait bounds the whole.
Immediate eligibility uses admit_gb (the actual atomic ot-heavy admission capacity), not free_gb after subtracting
unstarted waiters. When no host can admit now, planned free_gb still chooses the memory queue.
Queue candidates must have enough total physical RAM after mandatory headroom for the request.
--priority die: a die-level job (die STA / GEN / GRT / die chain; owner 2026-10-15 23:50, 2026-10-18). On the host it
                 waits with OT_HEAVY_PRIO=die and is admitted AHEAD of tile gates and benches: a non-die request yields
                 only while a waiting die job fits the host NOW (it starts within seconds); a die job that does not fit
                 never holds memory idle (DIE-FIRST v2, ot-heavy-reconcile).  ot-run also sets it for commands matching
                 DIE_RE (see die_like()).
--crit: a critical job (token sims, die GRT50, die clock runs; COORD 11-01): CPUWeight=1000 on its scope and cpu.weight
         ~1000 on its containers (default 100), and it bypasses the host load cap (non-crit jobs wait while load1 >=
         LOAD_CAP_FACTOR x nproc, default 1.5).  Same as OT_RUN_CRIT=1.  Memory admission is unchanged.
         CRITGATE_1112: the load GATE (1.25 x nproc) holds a crit/direct job only above 2.0 x nproc, and ot-heavy lets at most 4
         such bypassing crit jobs run per host (LOAD_GATE_CRIT_FACTOR, CRIT_BYPASS_MAX in /etc/ot-heavy.conf).
--direct: a critical blocking job listed in ~/coord/lanes/NEAR-CLOSE.tsv (DIRECT_1107, OWNER 11-07): implies --crit, goes to the
         qualifying host with the most real free memory, and starts at once while real MemAvailable >= --mem + 32 GB, past the
         load cap, the past-peak/reservation charge and DIE-RESERVE.  Disk floors, held hosts and MemoryMax stay.  Running jobs
         are never touched.  Without that memory on any host it waits and re-places.  Not for new builds.
--max-wait SEC: give up (exit 75, NOT RUN) when the host has not admitted the command after SEC seconds.

Exit status (v2, 2026-10-13, after a bench was reported "EXIT 0" although nothing had run): ot-run returns the command's
status ONLY when the remote side reported both the start (ot-heavy "admitted ... OT_RUN_START") and the exit of the
command.  Anything else -- ssh dropped, waiter killed, cd failed, no memory within --max-wait -- is a non-zero status and
a last stderr line "ot-run: NOT RUN ..." / "ot-run: NOT COMPLETED ...".  The last stderr line of a real run is
"ot-run: RAN on <host>: exit <n>".  Read that line when the call sits in a pipe (`| tail` hides the status).
A killed ot-run (timeout, Ctrl-C) cancels its waiter on the host, so the command cannot start later with nobody
listening; a command that already started is left running and the message says so.
SPILL (v8, FLEET-NEW2 2026-10-19: at 02:25 ot-epyc4 had 14 waiters / 364 GB and ot-epyc5 4 / 652 GB while ot-epyc6/7
admitted >1.1 TB each: every waiter named a --dir that existed on ONE host, so REBALANCE (len(qualifying) > 1) never
applied): when no host that has --dir admits the request now, ot-run copies the lane root (the --dir's
/srv/opentallas-scratch*/claude/<lane> directory, else --dir itself; <= OT_RUN_SPILL_MAX_GB, default 40, no absolute
symlink or /srv path in the command outside it) from the queue host to a big EPYC that admits the request NOW (same choose():
die jobs avoid throughput/Zen-3 hosts; tile work goes to throughput hosts first; a lane root holding -march=native builds
spills only to AVX-512 hosts), runs it there, and copies every file the run wrote back to the source host
(tar --keep-newer-files both ways, so newer files on either side are never clobbered).  The copy carries a marker
(.ot-run-spill-copy): normal placement ignores marked copies, so later jobs of the lane never run on stale data
without a fresh sync.  --hosts (explicit pin), --no-spill or OT_RUN_NO_SPILL=1 disable it.
SPILL v10 (OTRUN-SPILL 2026-10-19, MU-SLICE-GATE bug: "SPILL copy FAILED" yet the job ran on the spill host and its result
was never copied back): (1) GNU tar 1.35 with --keep-newer-files fails every directory that already exists, so every spill
of a lane to a host holding an earlier copy "failed" -- that error is now forgiven for paths that are directories, all
