command names inside a staged path; an absolute symlink out of the staged paths, > OT_RUN_SPILL_MAX_GB, or a staged
path that exists UNMARKED on the spill host (real data there) refuses the spill (the job then waits as before).  A lane
root that the stage creates on the spill host is marked too, and normal placement ignores a --dir any of whose
ancestors up to the lane root is marked, so later jobs never run on a staged copy by accident.  Without --stage
nothing changes.  Example: ot-run --mem 36 --dir $K/run --stage $K --stage $LANE/run_kit_r.sh -- bash $LANE/run_kit_r.sh $K route 36 8
OT_SCRATCH (v8): the job's environment carries OT_SCRATCH = the host's /srv/opentallas-scratch* volume with the most
free space; put NEW lane / job / die-chain directories under $OT_SCRATCH/claude/<lane>.
SCRATCH2 (v12, SCRATCH2-1021 2026-10-21, owner "definitely the tools should write both scratch disks to leverage space":
